@misc{Łukawski_24215,
 abstract = {Ani(mate) for piano and strings, composition commissioned by Festival Chamber Music at Lundsgaard (World premiere: August 7th 2025, Chamber Music at Lundsgaard, Kerteminde, Denmark, Second performance: August 12th 2025, Mogens Dahl Concert Hall, Copenhagen) Performers: the members of Trio Con Brio Copenhagen, Marmen Quartet, Enrico Pace, Hélène Clément, Michael Germer, Andrej Bielow, and Jonathan Swensen.

Program note:

Ani(mate), six preludes for strings and piano, was composed through a compositional process
involving interaction with artificial intelligence tools. The piece emerged from cycles of generating,
selecting, editing, and further developing musical materials suggested by AI models. Through this
iterative approach, initial musical ideas were continuously reshaped and refined, gradually defining
the character and structure of each prelude.
Various AI tools and techniques were employed during the compositional process and models used
include the use of various Large Language Models for decision making, linked with a symbolic AI
structure based on Performative Transactions—a new system enabling interconnected musical
structures to respond and transform in dialogue with each other, shaping a form of a posthumanist
intelligent musical system (as described in my PhD dissertation). Additionally, Google Magenta's
Performance RNN model generated some of the preliminary musical gestures and phrases.
Rather than delegating decisions entirely to automated processes, the composition relied on constant
evaluation, selection, and manual refinement. The resulting six preludes reflect this layered working
method.
The title, Ani(mate), carries multiple meanings. To "animate" is to bring to life, to give motion or
spirit. The term "mate" suggests companionship or partnership, yet simultaneously references
"checkmate" evoking notions of conflict, strategy, or finality—as in the decisive end of a chess match.
